{
  "teamId": 112,
  "year": 2024,
  "narrative": "The 2024 Chicago Cubs were a team that spent most of the year fighting themselves as much as their opponents. They opened the season with genuine promise — a strong April at 17-10 suggested this could be a year when everything clicked — but the bullpen, particularly Adbert Alzolay and Héctor Neris, consistently undermined whatever momentum the offense tried to build. The rotation showed flashes of brilliance, and hitters like Ian Happ, Michael Busch, and Cody Bellinger delivered timely power, but the Cubs could never string together enough clean baseball to distance themselves from the mediocre middle of the National League.\n\nMay and June brought the season's darkest chapter. The Cubs went 10-18 in May and 11-16 in June, a prolonged stretch of futility defined by late-inning collapses. Héctor Neris became a symbol of the team's bullpen woes, surrendering critical runs at the worst possible moments — blown leads against the Brewers, the Giants, and the Rays that turned winnable games into damaging losses. The offense offered occasional sparks, including Bellinger's three-run blast and Happ's home run against Colorado, but they were oases in a desert of inconsistency.\n\nJuly offered modest stabilization at 13-12, hinting that the club had steadied itself. Then August arrived and the Cubs caught fire, going an impressive 18-8 — their best month by far. Christian Bethancourt's deflected single and clutch doubles from Dansby Swanson and Mike Tauchman typified a month where things finally broke their way. The pitching tightened, the offense ran efficiently, and for a few weeks Wrigley Field buzzed with genuine playoff energy.\n\nBut September extinguished those dreams. The Cubs went 13-13 down the stretch, and the big moments of the final weeks were almost uniformly painful — Porter Hodge surrendering a go-ahead homer, Elly De La Cruz tripling into a season-killing loss in the final days. The Cubs finished 83-79, a record that captures a team talented enough to win but too inconsistent to matter when October arrived. The bullpen's volatility and the team's inability to hold leads in critical spots ultimately defined a year full of promise that quietly slipped away.",
